From: sunshineinabox <aqemail@gmail.com>
Subject: [PATCH] layer: bound the FIFO queue

The layer gives the driver MAILBOX and implements FIFO through the
gamescope_swapchain protocol, so a game's swapchain images are the only
limit on how far it runs ahead. With 3 images, a game that renders
faster than the panel keeps 2 frames waiting in gamescope and blocks in
vkAcquireNextImageKHR after it has already sampled its input. On the RG
55G1 (61 Hz) that was 32 ms from present to scanout, plus 15 ms blocked,
and 113 ms from an input to the panel under a 30 fps cap.

vkQueuePresentKHR on a FIFO swapchain now returns only once no more than
one of its frames is still waiting to be latched, so the app blocks
before it samples its next input instead of after. GAMESCOPE_WSI_FIFO_QUEUE
sets the limit (0-3, or "off" for the old behaviour), and
GAMESCOPE_WSI_FIFO_QUEUE_LIMITED sets it while gamescope's frame limiter
is active. That one also defaults to 1: with 0, a game needing 31 ms per
frame under a 30 fps cap ran at 20 fps instead of 30.

A present the app does not time gets an internal serial through
set_present_timing, which gamescope reports when the frame is latched or
discarded, for windows it does not show as well. Presents using
VK_GOOGLE_display_timing are left alone: that protocol reports no
discarded frames. The wait starts with the swapchain's first report
(gamescope reports nothing before it shows the window) and gives up
after 100 ms.

